Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Calibrate Force Sensor (Load Cell)", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9354320996901608`*^9, 
  3.935432108890316*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4c8cf27e-b0ad-4b76-8eba-\
c2955827f848"],

Cell["\<\
Use the TinkerForge Viewer to calibrate the force sensor. The calibrated \
value will be saved in the bricklet for subsequent use.
Select the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Calibration\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] button \
and press \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Calibrate Zero\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] with \
nothing touching the force sensor. Now put a 1 kg mass on the sensor and \
enter 10,000,000 (as 10000000) and press \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Calibrate \
Weight\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. The reason we use 10,000,000 rather than \
1,000 is because we wish to have the force sensor make measurements in 0.1 \
milligram force increments rather than grams. We just have to remember this \
when we subsequently convert force sensor measurements to Newtons (N).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9354321181463223`*^9, 3.935432246624823*^9}, {
  3.935432285931223*^9, 3.935432451562941*^9}, {3.9488906797200108`*^9, 
  3.9488906810132*^9}, {3.9803562796456013`*^9, 3.9803563202908707`*^9}, {
  3.980356443382773*^9, 
  3.980356444219596*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"847a14f8-a632-4724-8441-\
1c48ca091157"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Thermocouple", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9489032332984343`*^9, 3.948903235682218*^9}, 
   3.948907388429104*^9},ExpressionUUID->"18ae6983-cb02-429a-9226-\
b2999c36fd8c"],

Cell["\<\
The TinkerForge Thermocouple Bricklet supports all the main thermocouple \
types:  B, E, J, K, N, R, S and T . The most frequently used thermocouples \
are the Type K and J. Thermocouples are made by welding two different alloys \
together to form a bead. The ones used in class are type K and have a bead \
diameter of about 1 mm. Thermocouples are available with bead diameters of \
100 \[Mu]m (fairly common) and less. The smaller the bead diameter the faster \
the response to changing temperature. The mV level voltage generated by the \
welded alloys is converted to a temperature in digital form within the \
TinkerForge Thermocouple Bricklet. Each thermocouple type has a different \
nonlinear voltage to temperature relation which is usually represented as a \
high order polynomial. The Bricklet also determines which thermocouple is \
inserted in to it and invokes the appropriate voltage to temperature \
polynomial. 
It is important to note that Thermocouples should not be considered as \
precision measurement devices and rarely do better than \[PlusMinus]1 \
\[SmallCircle]C without calibration. Consider using a platinum resistive \
temperature sensor for more accurate temperature measurements.
A typical thermocouple connector is polarized. The +ve part of the connector \
is 2.5 mm wide and the other is 3 mm wide. Be aware of this when inserting.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.948903244642054*^9, 3.948903397328732*^9}, {
   3.948903498327836*^9, 3.948903685831308*^9}, {3.9489037284060097`*^9, 
   3.9489037403893967`*^9}, {3.948903783849971*^9, 3.948904090246504*^9}, {
   3.948904120859165*^9, 3.9489041960610647`*^9}, 3.948995031684157*^9, {
   3.94906741823897*^9, 3.949067452971842*^9}, {3.9490676846446533`*^9, 
   3.9490677009876423`*^9}, {3.94906773185877*^9, 
   3.9490678076378803`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"19d9169f-65e1-4fb3-89dc-\
a892e4db99d9"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Connect to TinkerForge", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.914517403800287*^9, 3.914517420488479*^9}, 
   3.918155130537187*^9, {3.918155255094969*^9, 
   3.9181552566867847`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9ba882f1-1b0e-42dd-9b21-\
1a808719aacd"],

Cell["\<\
In order to communicate with the TinkerForge Bricks and Bricklets from \
Mathematica you need to add the TinkerForge.dll file to your system.
To do this, if you have not already done so you need to go to:
 https://www.tinkerforge.com/en/doc/Software/API_Bindings_Mathematica.html#\
api-bindings-mathematica
 and click on the ZIP file link.
 Download it and open it and Copy the TinkerForge.dll file.
 Then go to your Wolfram directory and navigate to the NETLink subdirectory. \
On the Mac it is at:
 /Applications/Wolfram.app/Contents/SystemFiles/Links/NETLink
and Paste the TinkerForge.dll file.
You will need to restart Mathematica.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9489068549163017`*^9, 3.948907356646811*^9}, {
  3.948908630767873*^9, 3.948908662427725*^9}, {3.948908708045073*^9, 
  3.9489087084924593`*^9}, {3.980350100137453*^9, 
  3.980350108997967*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"232c2eb5-8479-4f6c-a620-\
221728d688e5"],

